LEARN.course({id:"rock",family:"Rock",name:"Classic and hard rock",tagline:"Riff-driven guitar music built on power chords, the blues scale and a loud British valve amp.",level:"Beginner",era:"1960s to today",artists:["Cream","The Who","Led Zeppelin","Deep Purple","AC/DC","Aerosmith","Van Halen"],listen:["The Kinks – You Really Got Me","Cream – Sunshine of Your Love","Led Zeppelin – Communication Breakdown","Deep Purple – Smoke on the Water","AC/DC – Back in Black"],key:"A major",bpm:120,meter:"4/4",techniques:["palm-muting","bends","vibrato"],sources:[{id:"s1",title:"Hard rock",site:"Wikipedia",url:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hard_rock",kind:"encyclopedia"},{id:"s2",title:"Making Sense of Rock's Tonal Systems",author:"Walter Everett",site:"Music Theory Online 10.4",year:2004,url:"https://www.mtosmt.org/issues/mto.04.10.4/mto.04.10.4.w_everett.php",kind:"paper"},{id:"s3",title:"Power chord",site:"Wikipedia",url:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Power_chord",kind:"encyclopedia"},{id:"s4",title:"Link Wray",author:"Rock & Roll Hall of Fame Library and Archives",site:"Rock & Roll Hall of Fame",year:2023,url:"https://library.rockhall.com/link_wray",kind:"article"},{id:"s5",title:"Adding the Major Third to Minor Pentatonic and Blues Scale Phrases",site:"Guitar World",url:"https://guitarworld.com/lessons/adding-major-third-minor-pentatonic-and-blues-scale-phrases",kind:"lesson"},{id:"s6",title:"Rock beat",author:"Team Soundbrenner",site:"Soundbrenner",url:"https://www.soundbrenner.com/blogs/articles/rock-beat",kind:"article"},{id:"s7",title:"Song structure",site:"Wikipedia",url:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Song_structure",kind:"encyclopedia"},{id:"s8",title:"Marshall Super Lead",site:"Wikipedia",url:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marshall_Super_Lead",kind:"encyclopedia"},{id:"s9",title:"The amps that changed music: JCM800",site:"Marshall",url:"https://www.marshall.com/ae/en/backstage/history-of/the-amps-that-changed-music-jcm800",kind:"article"},{id:"s10",title:"The History and Resurgence of P.A.F. Pickups",author:"Adam Goetz",site:"Seymour Duncan",year:2022,url:"https://www.seymourduncan.com/blog/latest-updates/resurgence-of-paf-pickups",kind:"article"},{id:"s11",title:"Five myths about Angus Young's AC/DC guitar gear that people still believe",author:"Justin Beckner",site:"Guitar.com",year:2021,url:"https://guitar.com/features/opinion-analysis/angus-young-ac-dc-guitar-gear-myths",kind:"article"},{id:"s12",title:"Marshall 1959 Super Lead Pedal Review",author:"Ted Drozdowski",site:"Premier Guitar",year:2025,url:"https://www.premierguitar.com/gear/reviews/marshall-pedamarshall-1959-super-lead",kind:"article"},{id:"s13",title:"Songwriting 101: basic song structure",author:"Yousician team",site:"Yousician",url:"https://yousician.com/blog/songwriting-101-basic-song-structure",kind:"lesson"}],study:[{title:"The Foundations of Rock",kind:"book",author:"Walter Everett",note:"A detailed, example-heavy guide to how rock harmony, form and guitar parts work, by the theorist behind the tonal-systems paper."},{title:"The Musical Language of Rock",kind:"book",author:"David Temperley",note:"Explains rock harmony, melody and form in plain terms, with many song examples you can check by ear."},{title:"Guitar Soloing For Beginners",kind:"course",author:"TrueFire",url:"https://learn.truefire.com/guitar-soloing-for-beginners",note:"Builds the A minor pentatonic box at the 5th fret into usable solo phrases, the same box this course uses."},{title:"Vintage Vault: 1966 Marshall JTM45",kind:"article",author:"Premier Guitar",url:"https://www.premierguitar.com/pro-advice/vintage-vault/1966-marshall-jtm45-amp",note:"A close look at the Marshall that came before the Plexi, useful for understanding where the British crunch started."},{title:"Angus Young's guitar gear: everything you need to nail the AC/DC star's high-voltage tones",kind:"article",author:"Guitar World",url:"https://guitarworld.com/features/angus-youngs-guitar-gear-everything-you-need-to-nail-the-acdc-stars-high-voltage-tones",note:"A gear walk-through for the classic humbucker-into-Marshall rhythm sound."},{title:"Led Zeppelin II",kind:"album",author:"Led Zeppelin",note:"Blues-rock riffs, open-string power chords and bent pentatonic solos, all in one record."},{title:"Back in Black",kind:"album",author:"AC/DC",note:"A study in space: simple open chords, a hard backbeat and room between every hit."}],steps:[{id:"sound",title:"Where hard rock comes from",body:"Hard rock grew out of the blues. In the 1950s, electric blues guitarists experimented with driving rhythms, distorted solos and power chords. In the 1960s, British and American bands such as the Kinks, the Who, Cream and the Jimi Hendrix Experience pushed that sound louder and heavier. By the 1970s, Led Zeppelin, Deep Purple, AC/DC and Aerosmith had set the template that many rock bands still follow.\n\nThe guitar does two jobs. It repeats a riff that carries the song, then steps out as a solo voice. The drums push with a strong kick and a backbeat on the snare, and the bass mostly locks with the drums underneath the guitars.\n\nAs you listen to the recordings below, notice three things: how short and repeatable each riff is, how much space sits between the guitar hits, and how the solos lean on bent blues-scale notes.",cites:["s1","s4"],blocks:[{type:"callout",tone:"history",text:"Robert Palmer named the 1950s blues guitarists Willie Johnson and Pat Hare as the true originators of the power chord. Link Wray's 1958 instrumental 'Rumble', all reverberating power chords and raw distortion, made the sound famous and inspired the Who and Led Zeppelin. The Kinks later built the hit 'You Really Got Me' around power chords.",cites:["s3","s4"]}]},{id:"harmony",title:"Power chords and borrowed chords",body:"A power chord is a root and a fifth with no third, so it is neither major nor minor. Under heavy distortion that bare fifth stays clear where a full triad can turn muddy, which is why rock leans on it so much.\n\nThe rest of rock harmony comes from the blues. Music theorist Walter Everett describes a rock system where each note of the minor pentatonic scale can carry its own major chord or power chord. In A that gives A, C, D, E and G: I, bIII, IV, V and bVII. These chords work outside classical rules of harmony and voice leading: they circle the tonic the way a riff does.\n\nThe bVII is the one you will use most. In A it is G. Everett shows it working as a neighbour to I (A G A), in the double-plagal move bVII IV I (G D A), and as a stand-in for V at a cadence. Adding bVI gives you a rising bVI bVII I lift that works well into a chorus.",cites:["s2","s3"],blocks:[{type:"shapes",title:"Power chords in A",shapes:[{name:"A5",frets:"x 0 2 2 x x"},{name:"C5",frets:"x 3 5 5 x x"},{name:"D5",frets:"x 5 7 7 x x"},{name:"E5",frets:"0 2 2 x x x"},{name:"F5",frets:"1 3 3 x x x"},{name:"G5",frets:"3 5 5 x x x"}],note:"Root on the low E or A string, fifth two frets up on the next string. The open A5 and E5 let you change chords fast."},{type:"progression",name:"Mixolydian vamp",chords:"I bVII IV I",note:"A G D A. The G to D to A move is the double-plagal cadence Everett traces through many 1960s rock songs.",cites:["s2"]},{type:"progression",name:"Pentatonic power chords",chords:"I5 bIII5 IV5 I5",note:"A5 C5 D5 A5. Every chord root comes from the A minor pentatonic scale, even though the chords are power chords.",cites:["s2"]},{type:"progression",name:"Chorus lift",chords:"bVI5 bVII5 I5 -",note:"F5 G5 A5. The bVII to I step stands in for V to I, a common rock cadence.",cites:["s2"]}]},{id:"rhythm",title:"The rock beat",body:"The most common rock beat puts the kick drum on beats 1 and 3, the snare on 2 and 4, and steady eighth notes on the hi-hat. The snare hits on 2 and 4 are the backbeat, and a rock rhythm guitar part is built to lock with them.\n\nHard rock drumming adds a strong bass drum and a loud backbeat, with cymbals for emphasis. A simple way to lift a chorus is to move the right hand from the closed hi-hat to the ride or an open hat. A half-time feel, with the snare only on beat 3, makes a bridge or an ending feel heavier without changing the tempo.\n\nLoad the verse groove under your riff. The extra kick on the 'and' of 3 pushes into beat 4, which is a good place for a chord change or a short fill.",cites:["s6","s1"],blocks:[{type:"groove",groove:{id:"rock-verse-drive",name:"Driving rock verse",role:"Verse",steps:16,beats:4,meter:"4/4",swing:0,t:{cr:"x...............",hh:"x.x.x.x.x.x.x.x.",sn:"....x.......x...",kk:"x.......x.x....."}},note:"Kick on 1 and 3 with a push on the and of 3, snare on 2 and 4, eighth notes on the hi-hat.",cites:["s6"]},{type:"groove",id:"rock-ride",note:"The same backbeat with the ride cymbal: use it to lift the chorus."},{type:"groove",id:"halftime",note:"Snare on beat 3 only. Try it for an outro or a heavy bridge.",cites:["s6"]}]},{id:"riffs",title:"Riffs and palm muting",body:"A riff is a short, repeated figure that the whole song hangs on. Hard rock guitarists play repetitive riffs as the rhythm part, and the best ones mix chord hits with single notes from the minor pentatonic or blues scale and leave gaps so the drums can be heard.\n\nThe main riff below is in A. It alternates the open A5 with a quick blues move on the A string, C to C sharp, the minor third rising to the major third. It answers with G5 and D5, then turns around with C5, D5 and A5. Palm-muted chugs keep the low end tight; lift your palm for the accented chords so they ring.\n\nThe chorus riff uses open chords in the same key. Strum from the wrist, accent the pushes that land just before the beat, and let the chords ring. A tight, muted verse against a ringing chorus is a simple way to make the chorus feel bigger than the verse.",cites:["s1","s5","s7"],blocks:[{type:"technique",id:"palm-muting",note:"Mute the chugs between chord hits, then lift the palm on the accents so the contrast drives the riff."},{type:"tab",id:"rock-riff-main",title:"Main riff in A",instr:"guitar",bpm:120,grid:4,tone:"rock-crunch",notes:[[0,5,0,2,"D,acc"],[0,4,2,2,"D,acc"],[2,5,0,1,"pm,D"],[3,5,0,1,"pm,U"],[4,5,3,1,"D"],[5,5,4,1,"h"],[6,4,2,2,"D"],[8,5,0,2,"D"],[8,4,2,2,"D"],[10,6,3,2,"D"],[10,5,5,2,"D"],[12,5,5,4,"D,acc"],[12,4,7,4,"D,acc"],[16,5,0,2,"D,acc"],[16,4,2,2,"D,acc"],[18,5,0,1,"pm,D"],[19,5,0,1,"pm,U"],[20,5,3,1,"D"],[21,5,4,1,"h"],[22,4,2,2,"D"],[24,5,3,2,"D"],[24,4,5,2,"D"],[26,5,5,2,"D"],[26,4,7,2,"D"],[28,6,5,4,"D,acc"],[28,5,7,4,"D,acc"]],note:"Bar 1 answers A5 with G5 and D5; bar 2 turns around with C5, D5 and A5 at the 5th fret. The hammer-on from C to C sharp is the blues move."},{type:"tab",id:"rock-chorus-open",title:"Open-chord chorus riff",instr:"guitar",bpm:120,grid:2,tone:"rock-crunch",notes:[[0,5,0,2,"D,acc"],[0,4,2,2,"D,acc"],[0,3,2,2,"D,acc"],[0,2,2,2,"D,acc"],[0,1,0,2,"D,acc"],[2,5,0,1,"D"],[2,4,2,1,"D"],[2,3,2,1,"D"],[2,2,2,1,"D"],[2,1,0,1,"D"],[3,6,3,3,"U,acc"],[3,5,2,3,"U,acc"],[3,4,0,3,"U,acc"],[3,3,0,3,"U,acc"],[3,2,3,3,"U,acc"],[3,1,3,3,"U,acc"],[6,6,3,1,"D"],[6,5,2,1,"D"],[6,4,0,1,"D"],[6,3,0,1,"D"],[6,2,3,1,"D"],[6,1,3,1,"D"],[7,4,0,5,"U,acc"],[7,3,2,5,"U,acc"],[7,2,3,5,"U,acc"],[7,1,2,5,"U,acc"],[12,4,0,1,"D"],[12,3,2,1,"D"],[12,2,3,1,"D"],[12,1,2,1,"D"],[13,4,0,1,"U"],[13,3,2,1,"U"],[13,2,3,1,"U"],[13,1,2,1,"U"],[14,5,0,2,"D,acc"],[14,4,2,2,"D,acc"],[14,3,2,2,"D,acc"],[14,2,2,2,"D,acc"],[14,1,0,2,"D,acc"]],note:"A, G and D (I, bVII, IV). The G and the D land an eighth note early, a push that gives the riff its swagger."}]},{id:"solos",title:"Solos from the pentatonic box",body:"Rock solos are mostly built from the pentatonic and blues scales, the most common scales for soloing in rock and blues. In A, the first box of the minor pentatonic sits at the 5th fret and covers every string in one hand position.\n\nOver a major-key rock progression, the minor pentatonic gives you the bluesy flat third and flat seventh. Bending is what makes it sing: push the 7th-fret D on the G string up a whole step to E, or nudge the 5th-fret C up a half step toward C sharp so it agrees with the A chord underneath. Adding that major third to minor pentatonic phrases is a standard blues and rock move. Hard rock solos lean on string bending and a wide vibrato to carry over a loud band.\n\nRock songs often place a guitar solo after the middle chorus, over the chords of the verse or chorus. Learn the lick below, then improvise four bars over I5 - bVII5 IV5 using only box one.",cites:["s5","s1","s7"],blocks:[{type:"scale",root:"A",scale:"pentMin",view:"box",note:"Box one at the 5th fret. The roots are on the low E (5th fret), D (7th fret) and high E (5th fret) strings."},{type:"technique",id:"bends",note:"Bend the b3 a half step toward the major third and the 4th a whole step up to the 5th. Check the target pitch by fretting it first."},{type:"technique",id:"vibrato",note:"End phrases on a long note with a wide, even vibrato. It is the signature of a rock lead voice."},{type:"tab",id:"rock-solo-lick",title:"Box-one solo lick in A",instr:"guitar",bpm:120,grid:4,tone:"rock-crunch",notes:[[0,2,8,3,"b2"],[3,1,5,1],[4,2,8,1],[5,2,5,1,"p"],[6,3,7,2],[8,3,5,2,"b1"],[10,4,7,2],[12,3,7,4,"b2,v"],[16,1,8,2,"b2"],[18,1,5,2],[20,2,8,1],[21,2,5,1,"p"],[22,3,7,2],[24,3,5,1],[25,3,6,1,"h"],[26,4,7,6,"v"]],note:"Bends on G, C and D, a pull-off on the B string, and the C to C sharp hammer-on before landing on the root with vibrato."}]},{id:"tone",title:"Crunch from a cranked British amp",body:"The classic rock tone is a humbucker guitar into a loud British valve amp. Marshall's 100-watt Super Lead, model 1959, debuted in 1965 and defined the 'Plexi' sound. It had no master volume, so its grinding 'Marshall crunch' came from turning the whole amp up until the power section overdrove. Players often linked its two input channels with a short cable for extra gain and a thicker sound.\n\nMarshall's later master-volume heads, including the JCM800 2203 from 1981, were built to give that distorted, sustained sound at more practical volumes. Gibson's PAF humbucker, fitted to the Les Paul from 1957, keeps its clarity and touch sensitivity at higher gain, which made it a quick favourite of rock and blues players.\n\nMuch of the sound is in your hands. AC/DC's Angus Young moves between rhythm and lead with his guitar's volume knob, according to his tech, and the bite comes from hitting the strings hard at high volume. Set the card below, then play the main riff softly and then hard to hear the amp respond.",cites:["s8","s12","s9","s10","s11"],blocks:[{type:"tone",id:"rock-crunch",name:"Cranked British crunch",kind:"electric",guitar:"Les Paul or SG style solid body",pickups:"Bridge humbucker (PAF style)",strings:"10s",amp:"Loud British 100-watt head (Marshall Super Lead or JCM800 type) into a 4x12",settings:{gain:6,bass:5,mid:7,treble:6,presence:6,reverb:1,master:8},demo:"rock-riff-main",tips:["Plexi-style amps have no master volume, so the crunch comes from turning the amp up until the power section overdrives. A master-volume circuit (JCM800 type) gives a similar sound at lower volume.","Players often jumped the two input channels of a Plexi with a short cable to add gain and balance a brighter and a darker channel.","Ride the guitar's volume knob: a little below full for rhythm, full up for leads. Angus Young's tech describes him switching this way instead of using a pedal.","Pick hard. In AC/DC-style rhythm playing, much of the grind comes from attack and volume more than from the gain control.","Use the bridge humbucker. PAF-style pickups keep clarity and touch sensitivity at higher gain, so chords hold their shape."],cites:["s8","s12","s9","s10","s11"],note:"Try the riff with gain at 4 and pick hard, then gain at 8 and pick softly. The first usually sounds bigger."}]},{id:"form",title:"The riff-driven song map",body:"Most rock songs are built on verses and choruses, and in hard rock the riff ties them together. A typical song opens on the main riff, runs the verse and chorus twice, a guitar solo follows the middle chorus, and the chorus returns to finish.\n\nThe verse and chorus should contrast, and the chorus should carry more intensity. In the map below the verse sits on the palm-muted I5 bVII5 IV5 riff with the hi-hat groove, and the chorus lifts with bVI, bVII and I over the ride. The solo plays over the verse chords, which keeps it grounded while the guitar takes the lead.\n\nThe intro can simply be the riff played by the guitar, and a riff used through the song can come back as the outro, so the song starts and ends on its strongest idea. Use the map for your song, then change bar counts to fit your lyrics.",cites:["s7","s13","s1"],blocks:[{type:"form",name:"Classic hard rock map",sections:[{type:"intro",bars:4,chords:"I5 - bIII5 IV5",groove:"rock-verse-drive",fill:"f-snare8"},{type:"verse",bars:8,chords:"I5 - bVII5 IV5",groove:"rock-verse-drive",fill:"f-snare8"},{type:"chorus",bars:8,chords:"bVI5 bVII5 I5 -",groove:"rock-ride",fill:"f-toms"},{type:"verse",bars:8,chords:"I5 - bVII5 IV5",groove:"rock-verse-drive",fill:"f-snare8"},{type:"chorus",bars:8,chords:"bVI5 bVII5 I5 -",groove:"rock-ride",fill:"f-toms"},{type:"solo",bars:8,chords:"I5 - bVII5 IV5",groove:"rock-ride",fill:"f-snare16"},{type:"chorus",bars:8,chords:"bVI5 bVII5 I5 -",groove:"rock-ride",fill:"f-build"},{type:"outro",bars:4,chords:"I5 - bIII5 IV5",groove:"halftime",fill:"f-stop"}],note:"Intro and outro reuse the main riff; the solo sits after the second chorus over the verse chords.",cites:["s7","s13"]}]},{id:"together",title:"Put it together",body:"You now have every part of a classic rock song: a riff, a chord vocabulary built on the pentatonic scale, a driving backbeat, a solo box and a crunch tone. The last piece is a vocal line that fits around the riff.\n\nHard rock singers often sit high in their range and use a raspy, forceful delivery, so leave room for the voice. Sing in the gaps between riff hits, and keep the chorus line short enough to shout over the band. The chorus should be the most intense part of the song, so save your highest note for it.\n\nWork through the checklist, record a rough take of the whole map, and listen back for where the energy dips.",cites:["s1","s7","s13"],blocks:[{type:"lyrics",section:"chorus",prompt:"Write a four-line chorus around one short title phrase you could shout over a loud band. Put the title on the first and last line.",tips:["Keep lines short: four to seven syllables leaves room for the riff.","Use strong, open vowels on long notes so they carry.","Land the title on beat 1 of the bar where the chorus chord changes."]},{type:"checklist",items:["Play the main riff in time with the verse groove, palm-muting the chugs.","Write a verse riff and a chorus riff that use I, bIII, IV, bVII or bVI.","Improvise four bars over the verse chords using only box one of A minor pentatonic.","Use at least one bend and one held note with vibrato in your solo.","Dial a crunch tone and control rhythm and lead with your volume knob.","Build your song from the map and record one full take."]}]}]}),LEARN.course({id:"punk",family:"Rock",name:"Punk and pop-punk",tagline:"Fast, loud, short songs played with downstroked power chords and a do-it-yourself attitude.",level:"Beginner",era:"1970s to today",artists:["Ramones","Sex Pistols","The Clash","Buzzcocks","Discharge","Descendents","Green Day","Blink-182"],listen:["Ramones – Blitzkrieg Bop","Sex Pistols – Anarchy in the U.K.","The Clash – Career Opportunities","Discharge – Why (EP)","Green Day – Dookie (album)"],key:"E major",bpm:176,meter:"4/4",techniques:["palm-muting","string-muting"],sources:[{id:"s1",title:"Punk rock",site:"Wikipedia",url:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Punk_rock",kind:"encyclopedia"},{id:"s2",title:"How to Jam Out Like the Ramones",author:"Aurelien Budynek",site:"Premier Guitar",year:2018,url:"https://www.premierguitar.com/lessons/how-to-jam-out-like-the-ramones",kind:"lesson"},{id:"s3",title:"Learn how to play the guitar like Johnny Ramone",author:"Tyler Golsen",site:"Far Out",year:2022,url:"https://faroutmagazine.co.uk/learn-how-to-play-the-guitar-like-johnny-ramone/",kind:"article"},{id:"s4",title:'"I love that sound": Kirk Hammett on how Johnny Ramone inspired his downpicking technique',author:"Crystal Koe",site:"Guitar.com",year:2023,url:"https://guitar.com/news/music-news/kirk-hammett-downpicking-inspiration-johnny-ramone/",kind:"interview"},{id:"s5",title:"Ramones",site:"Rock & Roll Hall of Fame",url:"https://www.rockhall.com/inductees/ramones",kind:"article"},{id:"s6",title:"Hardcore punk",site:"Wikipedia",url:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hardcore_punk",kind:"encyclopedia"},{id:"s7",title:"D-beat",site:"Wikipedia",url:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/D-beat",kind:"encyclopedia"},{id:"s8",title:"Pop-punk",site:"Wikipedia",url:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pop-punk",kind:"encyclopedia"},{id:"s9",title:"Rig Rundown: Green Day",author:"Chris Kies",site:"Premier Guitar",year:2013,url:"https://www.premierguitar.com/gear/rig-rundown-green-day",kind:"article"},{id:"s10",title:"Our Band Could Be Your Life: Scenes from the American Indie Underground, 1981-1991",author:"Michael Azerrad",site:"Hachette Book Group",url:"https://www.hachettebookgroup.com/titles/michael-azerrad/our-band-could-be-your-life/9781549115998/",kind:"book"},{id:"s11",title:"Rock beat",author:"Team Soundbrenner",site:"Soundbrenner",url:"https://www.soundbrenner.com/blogs/articles/rock-beat",kind:"article"},{id:"s12",title:"How to Palm Mute on Guitar for Perfect Rock and Metal Tone",site:"TrueFire",url:"https://truefire.com/learn/how-to-palm-mute-on-guitar",kind:"lesson"}],study:[{title:"How to Jam Out Like the Ramones",kind:"lesson",author:"Aurelien Budynek, Premier Guitar",url:"https://www.premierguitar.com/lessons/how-to-jam-out-like-the-ramones",note:"Twelve short examples covering downstroke rhythm, Ramones-style harmony and simple leads, written by a guitarist who toured with Marky Ramone."},{title:"Please Kill Me: The Uncensored Oral History of Punk",kind:"book",author:"Legs McNeil and Gillian McCain",note:"The New York scene in the musicians' own words, from the Stooges to the Ramones."},{title:"Our Band Could Be Your Life",kind:"book",author:"Michael Azerrad",url:"https://www.hachettebookgroup.com/titles/michael-azerrad/our-band-could-be-your-life/9781549115998/",note:"How 1980s hardcore and indie bands toured, recorded and released music themselves. The best guide to the DIY ethic in practice."},{title:"Commando: The Autobiography of Johnny Ramone",kind:"book",author:"Johnny Ramone",note:"Johnny Ramone on his playing, his gear and running a band with strict discipline."},{title:"Ramones",kind:"album",author:"Ramones",note:"The 1976 debut: short, fast songs of downstroked chords that set the template for punk and pop-punk."},{title:"Hear Nothing See Nothing Say Nothing",kind:"album",author:"Discharge",note:"The band the D-beat is named after. Listen to how the drums and buzzsaw guitars lock together."},{title:"Dookie",kind:"album",author:"Green Day",note:"Pop-punk at its most focused: power chords, palm-muted verses and big melodic choruses."}],steps:[{id:"sound",title:"Fast, loud and short",body:"Punk rock stripped rock and roll back down. Typical instrumentation is one or two guitars, bass, drums and vocals. Songs are shorter than in other rock styles and played at fast tempos, and complicated guitar solos were seen as self-indulgent. The Ramones, inducted into the Rock & Roll Hall of Fame as the band that pulled punk's loose elements together into a movement, kept their songs short, fast and built on a handful of chords.\n\nFrom New York the sound spread to London, where the Sex Pistols, the Clash and Buzzcocks took off in 1976 and 1977. In the 1980s hardcore bands played it faster, harder and more aggressively. In the 1990s, Californian bands such as Green Day and Blink-182 brought pop-punk, the melodic branch, back into the mainstream.\n\nWhat ties these together is attitude as much as sound. Punk prizes technical accessibility and a do-it-yourself spirit: if you can play a few power chords in time, you can write a punk song.",cites:["s1","s5","s2","s6","s8"],blocks:[{type:"callout",tone:"history",text:"Around the turn of 1977 the English fanzine Sideburns printed a drawing of three chord shapes captioned, in effect, 'here is a chord, here is another, here is a third: now form a band'. It became a slogan for punk's open-door approach.",cites:["s1"]}]},{id:"harmony",title:"Three chords and simple loops",body:"Punk harmony is mostly major-key rock and roll. Early punk songs kept the verse-chorus form of rock and roll, and Ramones-style harmony is mostly major and diatonic, built around the I, IV and V chords. Guitar parts use distorted power chords or full barre chords. Johnny Ramone often played full six-string barre chords where listeners assume power chords, which gives a fuller sound.\n\nPop-punk keeps the same tools and adds pop songcraft: catchy melodies over hard, fast guitars and drums, and sweet harmonies against rowdy riffs. A four-chord loop such as I V vi IV can carry a whole chorus while the melody does the work.\n\nIn E, I is E5, IV is A5 and V is B5. Keep the shapes movable so your fretting hand only slides between two or three positions and your pick hand can focus on time.",cites:["s1","s2","s3","s8"],blocks:[{type:"shapes",title:"Punk chord shapes in E",shapes:[{name:"E5",frets:"0 2 2 x x x"},{name:"A5",frets:"x 0 2 2 x x"},{name:"B5",frets:"x 2 4 4 x x"},{name:"C#5",frets:"x 4 6 6 x x"},{name:"A (barre)",frets:"5 7 7 6 5 5"},{name:"B (barre)",frets:"7 9 9 8 7 7"}],note:"Power chords for tight riffs, full barre chords for a bigger Ramones-style wall.",cites:["s2","s3"]},{type:"progression",name:"Three-chord verse",chords:"I5 - IV5 V5",note:"E5 E5 A5 B5. Two bars of the tonic, then IV and V push back to the top.",cites:["s2"]},{type:"progression",name:"Pop-punk chorus loop",chords:"I5 V5 vi5 IV5",note:"E5 B5 C#5 A5. Repeat it and let the melody change on top.",cites:["s8"]}]},{id:"rhythm",title:"The punk beat and the D-beat",body:"Punk drumming is fast and direct. Syncopation is much less common than in other forms of rock, and drums tend to sound heavy and dry. The basic punk beat is the rock beat played faster and harder, and at hardcore tempos drummers usually play straight eighth notes on the cymbals because smaller subdivisions get hard to play.\n\nThe D-beat comes from the English band Discharge. Their drummer Terry 'Tezz' Roberts developed it after a 1979 line-up change, and the name later stuck to a whole style that became especially popular in Sweden. It is a backbeat with a galloping kick figure underneath: kick, snare, kick, kick, snare. It sits well under fast power-chord riffs.\n\nUse the punk beat under your verses and choruses, and save the D-beat for a section that needs to hit harder.",cites:["s1","s6","s7","s11"],blocks:[{type:"groove",id:"punk",note:"The everyday punk beat: eighth notes, snare on 2 and 4, at a fast tempo.",cites:["s11"]},{type:"groove",groove:{id:"punk-dbeat",name:"D-beat",role:"Chorus",steps:8,beats:4,meter:"4/4",swing:0,t:{hh:"x.x.x.x.",sn:"..x...x.",kk:"x..x.x.."}},note:"Transcribed from the common D-beat pattern: the snare carries the backbeat while the kick adds the extra pushes that give it the gallop.",cites:["s7"]}]},{id:"downstrokes",title:"All downstrokes",body:"The engine of punk guitar is the downstroke. Johnny Ramone played downstrokes all the way through his songs, and said that before him people used them for brief stretches of a song, calling the technique a timing mechanism. Kirk Hammett of Metallica credits him as the inspiration for his own downpicking and describes the sound as brighter and less percussive.\n\nDownpicking eighth notes at punk tempos is tiring. Keep your arm relaxed and hanging naturally, let the wrist do the work, and warm up before you play fast. Start the exercises at a tempo where every stroke lands evenly, then raise it a few BPM at a time.\n\nPalm muting is the other half. Rest the fleshy edge of your picking hand across the strings at the bridge saddles for a tight chug in the verse, then lift it for the chorus so the chords open up. Light pressure gives a warmer tone that keeps its pitch, heavier pressure a tight, dry, percussive chug.",cites:["s3","s4","s2","s12"],blocks:[{type:"technique",id:"palm-muting",note:"Use it on verses and lift it for choruses. The same chords at the same tempo will feel twice as big."},{type:"technique",id:"string-muting",note:"Let the fretting hand ease off between chord changes so the gaps are silent and the next chord hits clean."},{type:"tab",id:"punk-verse-pm",title:"Palm-muted verse, all downstrokes",instr:"guitar",bpm:176,grid:2,tone:"punk-buzzsaw",notes:[[0,6,0,1,"pm,D,acc"],[0,5,2,1,"pm,D,acc"],[0,4,2,1,"pm,D,acc"],[1,6,0,1,"pm,D"],[1,5,2,1,"pm,D"],[1,4,2,1,"pm,D"],[2,6,0,1,"pm,D"],[2,5,2,1,"pm,D"],[2,4,2,1,"pm,D"],[3,6,0,1,"pm,D"],[3,5,2,1,"pm,D"],[3,4,2,1,"pm,D"],[4,6,0,1,"pm,D,acc"],[4,5,2,1,"pm,D,acc"],[4,4,2,1,"pm,D,acc"],[5,6,0,1,"pm,D"],[5,5,2,1,"pm,D"],[5,4,2,1,"pm,D"],[6,6,0,1,"pm,D"],[6,5,2,1,"pm,D"],[6,4,2,1,"pm,D"],[7,6,0,1,"pm,D"],[7,5,2,1,"pm,D"],[7,4,2,1,"pm,D"],[8,5,0,1,"pm,D,acc"],[8,4,2,1,"pm,D,acc"],[8,3,2,1,"pm,D,acc"],[9,5,0,1,"pm,D"],[9,4,2,1,"pm,D"],[9,3,2,1,"pm,D"],[10,5,0,1,"pm,D"],[10,4,2,1,"pm,D"],[10,3,2,1,"pm,D"],[11,5,0,1,"pm,D"],[11,4,2,1,"pm,D"],[11,3,2,1,"pm,D"],[12,5,2,1,"pm,D,acc"],[12,4,4,1,"pm,D,acc"],[12,3,4,1,"pm,D,acc"],[13,5,2,1,"pm,D"],[13,4,4,1,"pm,D"],[13,3,4,1,"pm,D"],[14,5,2,1,"pm,D"],[14,4,4,1,"pm,D"],[14,3,4,1,"pm,D"],[15,5,2,1,"pm,D"],[15,4,4,1,"pm,D"],[15,3,4,1,"pm,D"]],note:"E5 for a bar, then A5 and B5. Every stroke is a downstroke; accent beat 1 and keep the palm on the strings."},{type:"tab",id:"punk-chorus-open",title:"Open chorus on the pop-punk loop",instr:"guitar",bpm:176,grid:2,tone:"punk-buzzsaw",notes:[[0,6,0,1,"D,acc"],[0,5,2,1,"D,acc"],[0,4,2,1,"D,acc"],[1,6,0,1,"D"],[1,5,2,1,"D"],[1,4,2,1,"D"],[2,6,0,1,"D"],[2,5,2,1,"D"],[2,4,2,1,"D"],[3,6,0,1,"D"],[3,5,2,1,"D"],[3,4,2,1,"D"],[4,5,2,1,"D,acc"],[4,4,4,1,"D,acc"],[4,3,4,1,"D,acc"],[5,5,2,1,"D"],[5,4,4,1,"D"],[5,3,4,1,"D"],[6,5,2,1,"D"],[6,4,4,1,"D"],[6,3,4,1,"D"],[7,5,2,1,"D"],[7,4,4,1,"D"],[7,3,4,1,"D"],[8,5,4,1,"D,acc"],[8,4,6,1,"D,acc"],[8,3,6,1,"D,acc"],[9,5,4,1,"D"],[9,4,6,1,"D"],[9,3,6,1,"D"],[10,5,4,1,"D"],[10,4,6,1,"D"],[10,3,6,1,"D"],[11,5,4,1,"D"],[11,4,6,1,"D"],[11,3,6,1,"D"],[12,5,0,1,"D,acc"],[12,4,2,1,"D,acc"],[12,3,2,1,"D,acc"],[13,5,0,1,"D"],[13,4,2,1,"D"],[13,3,2,1,"D"],[14,5,0,1,"D"],[14,4,2,1,"D"],[14,3,2,1,"D"],[15,5,0,2,"D"],[15,4,2,2,"D"],[15,3,2,2,"D"]],note:"E5 B5 C#5 A5 with the palm lifted. Same downstrokes, same tempo, much bigger sound."}]},{id:"tone",title:"Raw, loud and simple",body:"Punk tone is mostly a guitar plugged into a loud, distorted amp. Hardcore guitarists play fast power chords through a heavily distorted, amplified tone that has been called a 'buzzsaw'. Johnny Ramone favoured Mosrites, a low-cost brand he could afford, and played them through Marshall 100-watt heads and 4x12 cabinets with no pedals at all. His tone, volume and distortion all came from the amps.\n\nIn pop-punk, Billie Joe Armstrong of Green Day plays through two 100-watt Marshall 1959 reissue heads, both modified for more gain. His first guitar, Blue, has its pickup selector locked on the bridge humbucker, his Les Paul Juniors carry a single P-90 in the bridge, and a Boss Blues Driver kicks in only for solos.\n\nA bridge pickup, a loud amp and downstrokes do most of the work. Too much gain smears fast eighth-note chords, so turn it down until each palm-muted chug is distinct. Set the card below and play the verse riff: the muted notes should bark and the open chords should roar.",cites:["s6","s3","s9"],blocks:[{type:"tone",id:"punk-buzzsaw",name:"Buzzsaw rhythm",kind:"electric",guitar:"Mosrite, Les Paul Junior or other simple solid body",pickups:"Bridge pickup only (P-90 style or humbucker)",strings:"10s",amp:"Loud British 100-watt head with extra gain (modified Marshall Super Lead type)",settings:{gain:8,bass:6,mid:6,treble:7,presence:6,reverb:0,master:7},pedals:["od"],demo:"punk-verse-pm",tips:["Use the bridge pickup. Billie Joe Armstrong keeps the selector on his first guitar, Blue, locked to the bridge humbucker.","Get the distortion from the amp. The hardcore 'buzzsaw' is fast power chords through a loud, heavily distorted amp.","Armstrong runs two 100-watt Marshall 1959 reissue heads modified for more gain and saves a Boss Blues Driver for solos, which is why the pedal here stays off for rhythm.","Johnny Ramone used no pedals: his tone and distortion came from his Marshall amps. Keep the chain short: guitar, cable, amp.","Downstrokes sound brighter and less percussive than alternate picking, so all-downstroke rhythm parts cut through without extra treble.","Back the gain off until every palm-muted eighth note is distinct at full tempo."],cites:["s9","s6","s3","s4"],note:"Play the verse riff, then switch on the overdrive and try a two-bar solo over E5. That is the Green Day approach."}]},{id:"form",title:"Short songs, no wasted bars",body:"Punk songs get in, say it and get out. Early punk kept rock and roll's verse-chorus form, but trimmed everything around it: songs are short and fast, and basic guitar breaks replace long solos.\n\nThe map below runs about a minute and a quarter at 176 BPM. It has a four-bar intro, two verse and chorus pairs, an eight-bar D-beat break, a last chorus and a hard stop. The verse uses the palm-muted I5 IV5 V5 riff and the chorus opens up on the pop-punk loop.\n\nIf your song feels long, cut a verse before you add a part. Ending on a hard stop, with the whole band hitting the last chord together, is a punk staple.",cites:["s1","s2"],blocks:[{type:"form",name:"Punk song map",sections:[{type:"intro",bars:4,chords:"I5 - IV5 V5",groove:"punk",fill:"f-snare8"},{type:"verse",bars:8,chords:"I5 - IV5 V5",groove:"punk",fill:"f-snare8"},{type:"chorus",bars:8,chords:"I5 V5 vi5 IV5",groove:"punk",fill:"f-snare16"},{type:"verse",bars:8,chords:"I5 - IV5 V5",groove:"punk",fill:"f-snare8"},{type:"chorus",bars:8,chords:"I5 V5 vi5 IV5",groove:"punk",fill:"f-snare16"},{type:"bridge",name:"D-beat break",bars:8,chords:"vi5 IV5 I5 V5",groove:"punk-dbeat",fill:"f-build"},{type:"chorus",bars:8,chords:"I5 V5 vi5 IV5",groove:"punk",fill:"f-stop"},{type:"outro",bars:2,chords:"I5 -",groove:"punk",fill:"f-stop"}],note:"About 54 bars: roughly 75 seconds at 176 BPM."}]},{id:"lyrics",title:"Direct lyrics, DIY ethic",body:"Punk lyrics are typically blunt and confrontational, and often about social and political issues: the Clash wrote about unemployment, and the Sex Pistols attacked the British political system. They are often shouted with a snarl rather than sung smoothly. Pop-punk turns the same directness toward lust, heartbreak, suburbia, boredom and rebellion, often with a joke attached.\n\nWrite the way you talk. Name the thing that annoys you or the person you miss, and say it in a line you could shout.\n\nThe DIY ethic is part of the style. Many punk bands produced their own records and released them on independent labels, and in the 1980s a whole network of bands, labels and fanzines grew up around punk's do-it-yourself credo. You can do the same with a phone, a cheap interface and an afternoon.",cites:["s1","s8","s10"],blocks:[{type:"lyrics",section:"chorus",prompt:"Write a chorus that states one complaint or one wish in plain words. Make the first line something you could shout with your friends.",tips:["One idea per chorus. If you need to explain it, it belongs in the verse.","Short words on fast notes: one syllable per eighth note at most.","Repeat the key line: repetition is what turns a line into a hook."]},{type:"callout",tone:"tip",text:"Record a demo the day you finish the song. Punk values getting it out over getting it perfect.",cites:["s10"]}]},{id:"together",title:"Put it together",body:"You have the parts of a punk song: three movable chords, a pop-punk loop for the chorus, the punk beat and the D-beat, all-downstroke rhythm, a buzzsaw tone and a short map. Now make it yours.\n\nWork through the checklist below. Keep the tempo honest: if your downstrokes fall apart, slow the song down and build stamina. The Ramones played much faster live than on their studio records, so speed is something you build with practice.\n\nWhen you have a full take, listen back for one thing: the palm-muted verse and the open chorus should feel clearly different even though the tempo never changes.",cites:["s2","s1"],blocks:[{type:"checklist",items:["Play the palm-muted verse riff with only downstrokes for a full minute without slowing down.","Switch between muted verse and open chorus without stopping.","Write a verse on I, IV and V and a chorus on a four-chord loop.","Put an eight-bar D-beat section somewhere in the song.","Write a chorus line you could shout, and repeat it.","Build the song from the map and keep it under two minutes."]}]}]}),LEARN.course({id:"emo",family:"Rock",name:"Emo and emo pop",tagline:"Confessional songs that swing from quiet, ringing guitars to loud, crunchy choruses.",level:"Intermediate",era:"1990s to 2010s",artists:["Rites of Spring","Sunny Day Real Estate","Jawbreaker","The Get Up Kids","Jimmy Eat World","Taking Back Sunday","Dashboard Confessional","My Chemical Romance"],listen:["Sunny Day Real Estate – Diary (album)","Jimmy Eat World – Clarity (album)","Jimmy Eat World – The Middle","The Get Up Kids – Something to Write Home About (album)","Taking Back Sunday – Tell All Your Friends (album)"],key:"G major",bpm:150,meter:"4/4",techniques:["palm-muting","string-muting","chord-embellishments"],sources:[{id:"s1",title:"Emo",site:"Wikipedia",url:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Emo",kind:"encyclopedia"},{id:"s2",title:"Emo pop",site:"Wikipedia",url:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Emo_pop",kind:"encyclopedia"},{id:"s3",title:"Clarity Turns 20",author:"Ian Cohen",site:"Stereogum",year:2019,url:"https://www.stereogum.com/2032965/jimmy-eat-world-clarity-turns-20/franchises/reviews/the-anniversary/",kind:"article"},{id:"s4",title:"Clarity (Jimmy Eat World album)",site:"Wikipedia",url:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clarity_(Jimmy_Eat_World_album)",kind:"encyclopedia"},{id:"s5",title:"Rig Rundown: Jimmy Eat World",author:"Perry Bean",site:"Premier Guitar",year:2017,url:"https://www.premierguitar.com/gear/rig-rundown-jimmy-eat-world",kind:"article"},{id:"s6",title:"Moss Icon: 'Complete Discography'",author:"Natty Nick",site:"VICE",year:2012,url:"https://vice.com/en/article/4rbp8r/moss-icon-complete-discography-album-review-temporary-residence",kind:"article"},{id:"s7",title:"Hardcore punk",site:"Wikipedia",url:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hardcore_punk",kind:"encyclopedia"},{id:"s8",title:"30 Essential Songs From The Golden Era Of Emo",author:"Stereogum staff",site:"Stereogum",year:2014,url:"https://www.stereogum.com/1685920/30-essential-songs-from-the-golden-era-of-emo/franchises/ultimate-playlist/",kind:"article"},{id:"s9",title:"5 Things Everyone Gets Wrong About Midwest Emo",author:"Rob Carroll",site:"Loudwire",year:2025,url:"https://loudwire.com/midwest-emo-explained/",kind:"article"},{id:"s10",title:"Rock beat",author:"Team Soundbrenner",site:"Soundbrenner",url:"https://www.soundbrenner.com/blogs/articles/rock-beat",kind:"article"}],study:[{title:"Nothing Feels Good: Punk Rock, Teenagers, and Emo",kind:"book",author:"Andy Greenwald",url:"https://openroadmedia.com/ebook/nothing-feels-good/9781466834927",note:"The standard history of emo from the DC scene to the early 2000s, written as it was happening."},{title:"Where Are Your Boys Tonight? The Oral History of Emo's Mainstream Explosion",kind:"book",author:"Chris Payne",note:"The bands of the 2000s mainstream wave describe how the songs and the scene came together."},{title:"40 Greatest Emo Albums of All Time",kind:"article",author:"Rolling Stone",url:"https://www.rollingstone.com/music/music-lists/40-greatest-emo-albums-of-all-time-23526/",note:"A listening map across every wave. Pick five and study how each one handles quiet and loud."},{title:"30 Essential Songs From The Golden Era Of Emo",kind:"article",author:"Stereogum",url:"https://www.stereogum.com/1685920/30-essential-songs-from-the-golden-era-of-emo/franchises/ultimate-playlist/",note:"A song-level playlist of the 1990s and early 2000s, good for learning arrangements by ear."},{title:"Diary",kind:"album",author:"Sunny Day Real Estate",note:"The 1994 Sub Pop record that defined second-wave emo dynamics and vocals."},{title:"Clarity",kind:"album",author:"Jimmy Eat World",note:"Two guitars, layered arrangements and confessional lyrics: the bridge from indie emo to emo pop."},{title:"Tell All Your Friends",kind:"album",author:"Taking Back Sunday",note:"Considered one of the most influential emo albums. Study how two singers and two guitars share the space."}],steps:[{id:"sound",title:"From DC hardcore to the mainstream",body:"Emo grew out of hardcore punk in Washington, DC, the birthplace of 'emotional hardcore'. Rites of Spring formed in 1983 and combined hardcore with melodic guitars and personal lyrics. Embrace and the 1985 'Revolution Summer' bands pushed the same way.\n\nIn the 1990s a second wave spread across the US. Sunny Day Real Estate merged emo with grunge in Seattle, and the loud-soft dynamics of songs like 'In Circles' became a blueprint for the era. Jawbreaker's Blake Schwarzenbach became the genre's first idol, and bands from the central US such as the Get Up Kids developed what was later called Midwest emo. Jimmy Eat World's Clarity (1999) became the high-water mark of pop-oriented emo, and Bleed American went platinum in 2002, carrying emo into the mainstream. Dashboard Confessional, Taking Back Sunday, My Chemical Romance and Fall Out Boy kept it there through the decade.\n\nThis course covers the second-wave and mainstream sound: soft and loud dynamics, ringing chords, hook-filled choruses and lyrics that read like diary entries. Midwest emo, the twinkly, math-rock-leaning branch, has its own course.",cites:["s1","s8","s3","s2"],blocks:[{type:"callout",tone:"tip",text:"Midwest emo is more a sound than a region. It is cleaner and mathier. Bands closer to punk and pop, the territory of this course, sound quite different.",cites:["s9"]}]},{id:"harmony",title:"Ringing open chords",body:"Emo grew from hardcore toward melodic guitars, and a simple way to get that melodic, ringing sound is to keep the same notes on the top strings while the bass moves. In G, the four shapes below all hold D and G on the B and E strings at the 3rd fret. G, Cadd9, Em7 and Dsus4 blend into one wash even as the chords change.\n\nThese add9, 7 and sus4 colours sound more open than plain triads, and they suit bittersweet lyrics. Play the loop clean and soft in a verse, then strum it hard with distortion in a chorus: you get the genre's soft-to-loud contrast without changing a note.\n\nEmo pop is known for concise songs and hook-filled choruses, so keep chord loops short. A vi IV I V loop under the chorus lets the melody carry the change.",cites:["s1","s8","s2"],blocks:[{type:"shapes",title:"Drone-string voicings in G",shapes:[{name:"G",frets:"3 2 0 0 3 3"},{name:"Cadd9",frets:"x 3 2 0 3 3"},{name:"Em7",frets:"0 2 2 0 3 3"},{name:"Dsus4",frets:"x x 0 2 3 3"}],note:"Ring and little finger stay on the 3rd fret of the B and E strings for all four chords."},{type:"technique",id:"chord-embellishments",note:"Add9, sus4 and 7th colours come almost for free when you keep the top strings fixed."},{type:"progression",name:"Ringing verse loop",chords:"I IVadd9 vi7 Vsus4",note:"G Cadd9 Em7 Dsus4, one bar each."},{type:"progression",name:"Chorus hook loop",chords:"vi IV I V",note:"Em C G D. Starting on the minor chord gives the chorus a bittersweet push.",cites:["s2"]}]},{id:"rhythm",title:"Steady verses, double-time choruses",body:"You can move a song between quiet and loud by switching drum feels at the same tempo. Musicologist Theo Cateforis describes emo song forms that swing between straight time and double time. A double-time feel makes the groove seem twice as fast while the tempo stays the same. A half-time feel, with the snare on beat 3, does the opposite and makes a part feel slow and heavy.\n\nThe verse groove below keeps the hi-hat steady and the kick sparse so clean guitars have room. The chorus groove switches to a double-time pattern: kick on every beat, snare on every 'and', with a ride or crash on the beat. Moving from one to the other at the same BPM gives a chorus a big lift.\n\nSave the half-time feel for the bridge, where the band can hit big chords and the singer can shout over them.",cites:["s10","s1"],blocks:[{type:"groove",groove:{id:"emo-verse",name:"Emo verse",role:"Verse",steps:16,beats:4,meter:"4/4",swing:0,t:{hh:"x.x.x.x.x.x.x.x.",sn:"....x.......x...",kk:"x.........x....."}},note:"A plain backbeat with a sparse kick, leaving space for arpeggios and vocals."},{type:"groove",groove:{id:"emo-doubletime",name:"Double-time chorus",role:"Chorus",steps:16,beats:4,meter:"4/4",swing:0,t:{cr:"x...............",rd:"x...x...x...x...",sn:"..x...x...x...x.",kk:"x...x...x...x..."}},note:"Kick and cymbal on every beat, snare on every and. Same BPM, twice the energy.",cites:["s10"]},{type:"groove",id:"halftime",note:"Snare on 3 only. Use it for the bridge or the last chorus.",cites:["s10"]}]},{id:"guitar",title:"Arpeggios, octaves and palm-muted builds",body:"Three guitar moves cover most of this style. The first is picked arpeggios: let each chord ring while you pick its notes one at a time. Early bands such as Moss Icon already set a jangle of guitars against sudden shifts between quiet and loud, and a ringing arpeggio is the simplest way to build the quiet side.\n\nThe second is the palm-muted chug. Ian Cohen points to the 'thrumming, palm-muted riffs' on Jimmy Eat World's Clarity, and muting is the easiest way to build tension before a chorus.\n\nThe third is the octave shape. Fret a note on the A string and the same note two frets higher on the G string, and let your index finger lightly touch the D string between them so it stays silent. Octaves let a lead line cut through a loud band without extra gain. With two guitars, as on Clarity, one player can hold the arpeggio while the other plays octaves on top.",cites:["s6","s3","s4"],blocks:[{type:"technique",id:"palm-muting",note:"Mute the last two bars before a chorus, then lift your hand on the downbeat."},{type:"technique",id:"string-muting",note:"Octave shapes depend on it: the fretting finger silences the string in the middle."},{type:"tab",id:"emo-drone-arp",title:"Ringing arpeggio verse",instr:"guitar",bpm:150,grid:2,ring:!0,notes:[[0,6,3,8],[1,4,0,4],[2,3,0,4],[3,2,3,2],[4,1,3,3],[5,2,3,3],[6,3,0,2],[7,4,0,1],[8,5,3,8],[9,4,2,4],[10,3,0,4],[11,2,3,2],[12,1,3,3],[13,2,3,3],[14,3,0,2],[15,4,2,1],[16,6,0,8],[17,4,2,4],[18,3,0,4],[19,2,3,2],[20,1,3,3],[21,2,3,3],[22,3,0,2],[23,4,2,1],[24,4,0,8],[25,3,2,4],[26,2,3,2],[27,1,3,3],[28,2,3,2],[29,3,2,2],[30,2,3,1],[31,1,3,1]],note:"G, Cadd9, Em7, Dsus4. The D and G on top never change, so the chords melt into each other."},{type:"tab",id:"emo-octave-lead",title:"Octave lead line",instr:"guitar",bpm:150,grid:2,tone:"emo-crunch",notes:[[0,5,5,2,"D"],[0,4,7,2,"x,D"],[0,3,7,2,"D"],[2,5,2,1,"D"],[2,4,4,1,"x,D"],[2,3,4,1,"D"],[3,5,5,1,"U"],[3,4,7,1,"x,U"],[3,3,7,1,"U"],[4,5,7,3,"D"],[4,4,9,3,"x,D"],[4,3,9,3,"D"],[7,5,5,1,"U"],[7,4,7,1,"x,U"],[7,3,7,1,"U"],[8,5,2,2,"D"],[8,4,4,2,"x,D"],[8,3,4,2,"D"],[10,6,3,2,"D"],[10,5,5,2,"x,D"],[10,4,5,2,"D"],[12,6,2,2,"D"],[12,5,4,2,"x,D"],[12,4,4,2,"D"],[14,5,5,2,"D"],[14,4,7,2,"x,D"],[14,3,7,2,"D"]],note:"D B D E D, B G F# D in octaves. Strum all three strings: the muted middle string just adds a click."}]},{id:"tone",title:"Crunchy but clear",body:"The emo rhythm tone sits between clean and heavy. Emo guitar uses both the softness and the loudness of punk, so you want enough drive for the chorus to hit hard and little enough that the open strings in a Cadd9 or Em7 still ring as separate notes. Too much gain turns those voicings to mush.\n\nA good starting point is a bridge humbucker or a thick single-coil into a moderately driven tube amp, with the guitar's volume knob and your picking strength doing the switching between verse and chorus. Jimmy Eat World began with a simple guitar-into-tube-amp setup, and Jim Adkins plays P-90-loaded guitars such as his Fender JA-90 Telecaster.\n\nLayering matters as much as gain. Adkins has described Clarity's arrangements growing by adding part after part, so if you record, double the chorus guitar with a different voicing instead of turning the gain up.",cites:["s1","s5","s4"],blocks:[{type:"tone",id:"emo-crunch",name:"Crunchy but clear",kind:"electric",guitar:"Telecaster style, SG or similar two-pickup guitar",pickups:"Bridge humbucker or P-90 style pickup",strings:"10s",amp:"Medium-gain tube amp (British or American style)",settings:{gain:5,bass:5,mid:6,treble:6,presence:5,reverb:2,master:6},pedals:["od","reverb"],demo:"emo-chorus-strum",tips:["Set the gain just past the point where chords break up, then check that every string of the Cadd9 still rings separately.","Let your pick hand set the dynamics: softly near the neck for verses, hard near the bridge for choruses.","Roll the guitar volume back for the verse and turn it up for the chorus instead of switching channels.","Jim Adkins of Jimmy Eat World plays P-90-loaded guitars such as his Fender JA-90 Telecaster, and the band started out plugging straight into tube amps.","Layer guitars when you record. Clarity's two guitars and stacked parts sound bigger than one guitar with more gain."],cites:["s1","s5","s4"],note:"Play the strum softly, then hard. The tone should go from glassy to growling without touching a knob."},{type:"tab",id:"emo-chorus-strum",title:"Loud chorus strum",instr:"guitar",bpm:150,grid:2,tone:"emo-crunch",notes:[[0,6,3,2,"D,acc"],[0,5,2,2,"D,acc"],[0,4,0,2,"D,acc"],[0,3,0,2,"D,acc"],[0,2,3,2,"D,acc"],[0,1,3,2,"D,acc"],[2,6,3,1,"D"],[2,5,2,1,"D"],[2,4,0,1,"D"],[2,3,0,1,"D"],[2,2,3,1,"D"],[2,1,3,1,"D"],[3,6,3,1,"U"],[3,5,2,1,"U"],[3,4,0,1,"U"],[3,3,0,1,"U"],[3,2,3,1,"U"],[3,1,3,1,"U"],[4,5,3,2,"D,acc"],[4,4,2,2,"D,acc"],[4,3,0,2,"D,acc"],[4,2,3,2,"D,acc"],[4,1,3,2,"D,acc"],[6,5,3,1,"D"],[6,4,2,1,"D"],[6,3,0,1,"D"],[6,2,3,1,"D"],[6,1,3,1,"D"],[7,5,3,1,"U"],[7,4,2,1,"U"],[7,3,0,1,"U"],[7,2,3,1,"U"],[7,1,3,1,"U"],[8,6,0,2,"D,acc"],[8,5,2,2,"D,acc"],[8,4,2,2,"D,acc"],[8,3,0,2,"D,acc"],[8,2,3,2,"D,acc"],[8,1,3,2,"D,acc"],[10,6,0,1,"D"],[10,5,2,1,"D"],[10,4,2,1,"D"],[10,3,0,1,"D"],[10,2,3,1,"D"],[10,1,3,1,"D"],[11,6,0,1,"U"],[11,5,2,1,"U"],[11,4,2,1,"U"],[11,3,0,1,"U"],[11,2,3,1,"U"],[11,1,3,1,"U"],[12,4,0,2,"D,acc"],[12,3,2,2,"D,acc"],[12,2,3,2,"D,acc"],[12,1,3,2,"D,acc"],[14,4,0,1,"D"],[14,3,2,1,"D"],[14,2,3,1,"D"],[14,1,3,1,"D"],[15,4,0,1,"U"],[15,3,2,1,"U"],[15,2,3,1,"U"],[15,1,3,1,"U"]],note:"The verse chords strummed hard: G, Cadd9, Em7, Dsus4, two beats each."}]},{id:"form",title:"Quiet verse, loud chorus, shouted bridge",body:"Emo pop favours concise songs with hook-filled choruses, and the arrangement does a lot of the emotional work. The map below opens on clean arpeggios, keeps the verse soft, builds through a short pre-chorus, and lets the chorus hit with double-time drums and distorted chords.\n\nThe bridge is where many songs break the pattern. Screamo, a more aggressive branch of emo, uses screamed vocals, and early bands such as Moss Icon already lurched between quiet and loud. Hardcore gave the scene gang vocals, where the band and often the crowd shout lines together. A half-time bridge with everyone shouting one line gives you that moment.\n\nAfter the bridge, bring the chorus back once more, then end on the same quiet figure you started with.",cites:["s2","s1","s6","s7","s10"],blocks:[{type:"form",name:"Emo pop song map",sections:[{type:"intro",bars:4,chords:"I IVadd9 vi7 Vsus4",groove:"emo-verse",fill:""},{type:"verse",bars:8,chords:"I IVadd9 vi7 Vsus4",groove:"emo-verse",fill:"f-snare8"},{type:"pre",bars:4,chords:"IVadd9 - Vsus4 -",groove:"build-snare",fill:"f-build"},{type:"chorus",bars:8,chords:"vi IV I V",groove:"emo-doubletime",fill:"f-toms"},{type:"verse",bars:8,chords:"I IVadd9 vi7 Vsus4",groove:"emo-verse",fill:"f-snare8"},{type:"pre",bars:4,chords:"IVadd9 - Vsus4 -",groove:"build-snare",fill:"f-build"},{type:"chorus",bars:8,chords:"vi IV I V",groove:"emo-doubletime",fill:"f-toms"},{type:"bridge",name:"Shouted bridge",bars:8,chords:"vi7 - IVadd9 -",groove:"halftime",fill:"f-build"},{type:"chorus",bars:8,chords:"vi IV I V",groove:"emo-doubletime",fill:"f-stop"},{type:"outro",bars:4,chords:"I IVadd9 vi7 Vsus4",groove:"emo-verse",fill:""}],note:"Quiet intro and verse, a palm-muted pre-chorus, a double-time chorus and a half-time shouted bridge."}]},{id:"lyrics",title:"Confessional, first person",body:"Emo lyrics are usually personal and confessional, often about failed romance, self-loathing and insecurity. AllMusic describes them as either free-associative poetry or intimate confessionals. Emo pop pairs those themes with bright, singable melodies, so a sad line often sits on an upbeat hook.\n\nWrite in the first person and speak to someone directly. Specific details make it believable: a street, a time on the clock, the thing you said in the car. Ian Cohen notes that on Clarity Jim Adkins wrote as himself instead of through characters, and that directness is the style.\n\nFor the bridge, find one short line the whole band could shout together, and repeat it.",cites:["s1","s2","s3"],blocks:[{type:"lyrics",section:"verse",prompt:"Write a verse in the first person to one person, set in one real place. Include a time, an object and something you wish you had said.",tips:['Use "I" and "you". Avoid explaining the feeling; describe the scene.',"One concrete detail per line beats two abstract ones.","Let the last line of the verse set up the chorus title."]},{type:"lyrics",section:"bridge",prompt:"Write a two-line bridge for the whole band to shout. Make it the bluntest thing in the song.",tips:["Keep it under eight syllables per line so a group can shout it together.","Repeat it at least twice, louder the second time."]}]},{id:"together",title:"Put it together",body:"You have the tools of emo pop: drone-string voicings, a verse groove and a double-time chorus, arpeggios, octaves and palm mutes, a crunchy but clear tone, a map built on dynamics and a confessional lyric. The checklist below walks you through building one song.\n\nWhen you play it back, listen for contrast first. If the chorus does not feel much bigger than the verse, make the verse quieter before you make the chorus louder. Then check the bridge: a shouted line works best when it is short, so cut it down until the whole band can deliver it together.",cites:["s1","s8","s2"],blocks:[{type:"checklist",items:["Play the four drone-string chords as a ringing arpeggio with no gaps between changes.","Write a verse loop and a separate chorus loop that starts on vi.","Switch from the verse groove to the double-time groove at the same BPM.","Add an octave line over the chorus with the middle string muted.","Set a tone that is clean when you pick softly and crunchy when you dig in.","Write a first-person verse and a shouted bridge, then build the song from the map."]}]}]}),LEARN.course({id:"midwest-emo",family:"Rock",name:"Midwest emo",tagline:"Twinkly, open-tuned clean guitars, mathy rhythms and quiet songs about the places you came from.",level:"Intermediate",era:"1990s to today",artists:["Cap'n Jazz","Braid","The Promise Ring","American Football","Algernon Cadwallader","Tiny Moving Parts","Modern Baseball"],listen:["American Football – Never Meant","American Football – American Football (1999 album)","Cap'n Jazz – Shmap'n Shmazz (album)","Algernon Cadwallader – Some Kind of Cadwallader (album)"],key:"F major",bpm:120,meter:"4/4",tuning:{name:"FACGCE",notes:"F2 A2 C3 G3 C4 E4"},techniques:["open-tunings","tapping","legato","natural-harmonics"],sources:[{id:"s1",title:"Midwest emo",site:"Wikipedia",url:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Midwest_emo",kind:"encyclopedia"},{id:"s2",title:"5 Things Everyone Gets Wrong About Midwest Emo",author:"Rob Carroll",site:"Loudwire",year:2025,url:"https://loudwire.com/midwest-emo-explained/",kind:"article"},{id:"s3",title:"Rig Rundown: American Football",author:"Perry Bean",site:"Premier Guitar",year:2023,url:"https://www.premierguitar.com/videos/rig-rundown/american-football",kind:"article"},{id:"s4",title:"American Football (1999 album)",site:"Wikipedia",url:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/American_Football_(1999_album)",kind:"encyclopedia"},{id:"s5",title:"List of guitar tunings",site:"Wikipedia",url:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_guitar_tunings",kind:"encyclopedia"},{id:"s6",title:"Brutalist Riffs: A Guide to Math Rock",author:"Erick Bradshaw",site:"Bandcamp Daily",year:2025,url:"https://daily.bandcamp.com/lists/brutalist-riffs-a-guide-to-math-rock",kind:"article"},{id:"s7",title:"Algernon Cadwallader",site:"Wikipedia",url:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Algernon_Cadwallader",kind:"encyclopedia"},{id:"s8",title:"Never Meant: The Complete Oral History of American Football",author:"T. J. Kliebhan",site:"VICE",year:2016,url:"https://www.vice.com/en/article/never-meant-the-complete-oral-history-of-american-football/",kind:"interview"},{id:"s9",title:"'American Football' Turns 20",author:"Nina Corcoran",site:"Stereogum",year:2019,url:"https://www.stereogum.com/2058678/american-football-turns-20/reviews/the-anniversary/",kind:"article"},{id:"s10",title:"The 150 Best Albums of the 1990s",author:"Pitchfork staff (American Football entry by Ian Cohen)",site:"Pitchfork",year:2022,url:"https://pitchfork.com/features/lists-and-guides/the-best-albums-of-the-1990s/",kind:"article"},{id:"s11",title:"Origins of Midwest emo",author:"Alicia Szczesniak",site:"The Post (Ohio University)",year:2023,url:"https://www.thepostathens.com/article/2023/09/origins-of-midwest-emo",kind:"article"},{id:"s12",title:"Gulfer Explain Their Brand of Emo Math Rock",author:"Eric Séguin",site:"VICE",year:2014,url:"https://www.vice.com/en_ca/article/r7p5nx/gulfer-interview",kind:"interview"},{id:"s13",title:"Some Kind of Cadwallader (review)",author:"Brian Shultz",site:"Punknews.org",year:2008,url:"https://www.punknews.org/review/7383",kind:"article"}],study:[{title:"American Football",kind:"album",author:"American Football",note:"The 1999 debut: every song in a different tuning, interlocking clean guitars and trumpet. Learn one song by ear before anything else."},{title:"American Football: Tiny Desk Concert",kind:"video",author:"NPR Music",url:"https://www.npr.org/2019/07/08/737950240/american-football-tiny-desk-concert",note:"A close, quiet view of the band playing live, good for watching how the guitar parts interlock."},{title:"Never Meant: The Complete Oral History of American Football",kind:"article",author:"T. J. Kliebhan, VICE",url:"https://www.vice.com/en/article/never-meant-the-complete-oral-history-of-american-football/",note:"The band explain how the songs, the trumpet and the tunings came together."},{title:"Math Rock",kind:"book",author:"Jeff Gomez",url:"https://www.stereogum.com/2257843/math-rock-jeff-gomez-excerpt/columns/sounding-board/book-club/",note:"A history of the odd-meter, tapping-heavy scene Midwest emo borrows from (link is an excerpt)."},{title:"30 Best Emo Revival Albums, Ranked",kind:"article",author:"SPIN",url:"https://www.spin.com/2017/06/best-emo-revival-albums-ranked/",note:"A listening list for the 2010s revival bands who took the twinkly sound further."},{title:"Some Kind of Cadwallader",kind:"album",author:"Algernon Cadwallader",note:"Tapping, harmonics, capoed chords and off-time rhythms at punk energy."},{title:"Shmap'n Shmazz",kind:"album",author:"Cap'n Jazz",note:"The raw, shouty Chicago record that much of the scene grew out of."}],steps:[{id:"sound",title:"Twinkle, math and the Midwest",body:"Midwest emo took shape in the American Midwest in the 1990s, around bands such as Cap'n Jazz, Braid, the Promise Ring, the Get Up Kids and American Football. It moved away from emo's hardcore punk roots toward indie rock and math rock, and many hear it as a much less intense version of emo.\n\nThe sound is built on arpeggiated melodies, cycling guitar parts, chugging bass lines and unconventional singing. American Football's 1999 debut, with its interlocking clean guitars and trumpet, is the record most players study, and its 'twinkly' guitar gave the style a nickname. Algernon Cadwallader led a revival in the late 2000s.\n\nToday the label describes a sound more than a place: the revival spread to Pennsylvania, New Jersey and beyond. If you came from the emo course, expect cleaner tones, odd tunings and busier guitar parts.",cites:["s1","s2","s4","s9","s13","s11"],blocks:[{type:"callout",tone:"history",text:"American Football recorded their debut in the last days before the members left college. They doubled and tripled the guitar tracks to thicken the sound.",cites:["s4"]}]},{id:"harmony",title:"Open tunings and maj9 chords",body:"Midwest emo leans on alternate tunings. Each song on American Football's debut is in a different tuning, and F A C G C E, low to high, is the best known: it is listed as the tuning of 'Never Meant', and it is often called the math rock tuning. Strum the open strings and you hear an Fmaj9 chord.\n\nThe tuning changes how you write. Open strings ring under almost every shape, so a one-finger chord sounds rich. Fretting the low string at the 5th fret gives a lydian-sounding Bbmaj7#11, and a few fretted notes give Dm9 or Gm7 with open strings shimmering on top. Players often add a capo to move these shapes to a new key.\n\nExpect major 7th, 9th and sus colours more than plain triads: the open strings add them for you. In American Football the two guitarists rarely play in the same open tuning, so each part brings its own set of ringing notes.",cites:["s4","s5","s3","s10","s7"],blocks:[{type:"tuning",name:"FACGCE",notes:"F2 A2 C3 G3 C4 E4",note:"From standard: raise the low E a half step to F, lower the D string a whole step to C, and raise the B string a half step to C. The A, G and high E strings stay where they are.",cites:["s5"]},{type:"technique",id:"open-tunings",note:"In FACGCE the open strings already make Fmaj9, so most shapes need only one or two fingers."},{type:"shapes",title:"Shapes in FACGCE",tuning:"F2 A2 C3 G3 C4 E4",shapes:[{name:"Fmaj9",frets:"0 0 0 0 0 0"},{name:"Bbmaj7#11",frets:"5 0 0 0 0 0"},{name:"Dm9",frets:"x 5 5 2 0 0"},{name:"Am7",frets:"x 0 0 0 0 0"},{name:"Gm7",frets:"2 1 2 0 2 1"},{name:"C",frets:"x x 0 0 0 0"}],note:"The top two open strings (C and E) ring through nearly every chord."},{type:"progression",name:"Open-string verse",chords:"Imaj9 IVmaj7#11 vi9 V",note:"Fmaj9 Bbmaj7#11 Dm9 C. Only the bass note and one or two fretted notes change."},{type:"progression",name:"Build",chords:"IVmaj7#11 - vi9 iii7",note:"Bbmaj7#11 Dm9 Am7. Sitting on IV keeps the build floating before it resolves."}]},{id:"rhythm",title:"Mathy rhythm and odd meters",body:"Math rock gives Midwest emo its rhythmic backbone: non-standard and frequently changing time signatures, and drums that carry much of the rhythmic weight. American Football wanted to sound more jazzy than loud and aggressive, and drummer Steve Lamos brought in his love of 1970s jazz fusion. On their debut, time-signature shifts skip around under light, delicate guitar parts.\n\nYou do not need odd meters everywhere. Try keeping the verse in 4/4 with a ride pattern and syncopated kicks that leave space, then switch to 7/8 for an interlude. The 7/8 groove below groups its seven eighth notes as 2 + 2 + 3.\n\nGuitar phrases in groups of three over a 4/4 beat give a similar off-kilter feel without changing the meter.",cites:["s6","s12","s8","s9"],blocks:[{type:"groove",groove:{id:"mwemo-verse",name:"Midwest verse",role:"Verse",steps:16,beats:4,meter:"4/4",swing:0,t:{rd:"x.x.x.x.x.x.x.x.",sn:"....x.....g.x...",kk:"x..x......x..x.."}},note:"Ride in eighths, a ghosted snare and a syncopated kick that leaves room for the guitars."},{type:"groove",groove:{id:"mwemo-seven",name:"7/8 interlude",role:"Bridge & feels",steps:14,beats:3.5,meter:"7/8",swing:0,t:{rd:"x.x.x.x.x.x.x.",sn:"....x.......x.",kk:"x.....x.x....."}},note:"Seven eighth notes grouped 2 + 2 + 3. Count it ONE-two THREE-four FIVE-six-seven.",cites:["s6"]},{type:"groove",id:"build-tom",note:"Tom-heavy build for the loud sections."}]},{id:"guitar",title:"Twinkly arpeggios and tapping",body:"Midwest guitar parts are busy and quiet at the same time. Players pick arpeggios across open-tuned strings and add hammer-ons, pull-offs and natural harmonics. Algernon Cadwallader's Joe Reinhart is known for finger-tapping, arpeggios, harmonics, hammer-ons and pull-offs in odd time signatures. Math rock guitarists lay tapped parts over drum patterns that rarely sit in plain 4/4, and some use loop pedals to stack parts on top of the drums.\n\nThe arpeggio below holds a bass note with the thumb while the fingers walk up the open strings, with a hammer-on and pull-offs on the high E. The tapping riff uses a finger of the picking hand to tap a high note, pull off to a fretted note, then to the open string, so three notes sound from one motion.\n\nAmerican Football built their songs around the interplay of two guitars, timing parts from musical cues, and Algernon Cadwallader's 2008 debut was praised for the same clever guitar interplay. If you have a second guitarist, write a part that answers yours instead of doubling it.",cites:["s7","s12","s6","s4","s13"],blocks:[{type:"technique",id:"tapping",note:"Tap with the middle finger so you can keep the pick in your hand between tapped phrases."},{type:"technique",id:"legato",note:"Hammer-ons and pull-offs against open strings are the core of the twinkly sound."},{type:"technique",id:"natural-harmonics",note:"In an open tuning, harmonics at the 12th, 7th and 5th frets all fit the open chord."},{type:"tab",id:"mwemo-twinkle-arp",title:"Twinkly arpeggio in FACGCE",instr:"guitar",tuning:"F2 A2 C3 G3 C4 E4",bpm:120,grid:4,ring:!0,tone:"mwemo-clean",notes:[[0,6,0,8,"P"],[2,3,0,4,"I"],[3,2,0,4,"M"],[4,1,0,1,"A"],[5,1,1,1,"h"],[6,2,0,2,"M"],[8,3,0,4,"I"],[10,1,3,1,"A"],[11,1,1,1,"p"],[12,1,0,2,"p"],[14,2,0,2,"M"],[16,6,5,8,"P"],[18,3,0,4,"I"],[19,2,0,4,"M"],[20,1,0,1,"A"],[21,1,1,1,"h"],[22,2,0,2,"M"],[24,5,5,8,"P"],[26,3,2,4,"I"],[27,2,0,4,"M"],[28,1,0,1,"A"],[29,1,1,1,"h"],[30,2,0,1,"M"],[31,3,2,1,"I"]],note:"Fmaj9 for a bar, then Bbmaj7#11 and Dm9. Thumb on the bass, fingers on the open strings, hammer and pull on the high E."},{type:"tab",id:"mwemo-tap-riff",title:"Tapped riff in FACGCE",instr:"guitar",tuning:"F2 A2 C3 G3 C4 E4",bpm:120,grid:4,tone:"mwemo-clean",notes:[[0,6,0,16],[0,1,8,1,"t"],[1,1,3,1,"p"],[2,1,0,1,"p"],[3,1,3,1,"h"],[4,1,10,1,"t"],[5,1,3,1,"p"],[6,1,0,1,"p"],[7,1,3,1,"h"],[8,1,12,1,"t"],[9,1,5,1,"p"],[10,1,0,1,"p"],[11,1,5,1,"h"],[12,1,8,1,"t"],[13,1,5,1,"p"],[14,1,1,1,"p"],[15,1,5,1,"h"],[16,6,5,16],[16,2,9,1,"t"],[17,2,5,1,"p"],[18,2,0,1,"p"],[19,2,5,1,"h"],[20,2,10,1,"t"],[21,2,5,1,"p"],[22,2,0,1,"p"],[23,2,5,1,"h"],[24,2,12,1,"t"],[25,2,7,1,"p"],[26,2,0,1,"p"],[27,2,7,1,"h"],[28,2,9,1,"t"],[29,2,7,1,"p"],[30,2,5,1,"p"],[31,2,2,1,"p"]],note:"Tap, pull off, pull off to the open string, hammer back. Bar 1 sits on the high E over the open F; bar 2 moves to the second string (tuned to C) over a Bb bass."},{type:"tab",id:"mwemo-harmonics",title:"Harmonic chime intro",instr:"guitar",tuning:"F2 A2 C3 G3 C4 E4",bpm:120,grid:2,ring:!0,tone:"mwemo-clean",notes:[[0,6,12,8,"nh"],[1,4,12,7,"nh"],[2,3,12,6,"nh"],[3,2,12,5,"nh"],[4,1,12,4,"nh"],[5,3,5,3,"nh"],[6,2,5,2,"nh"],[7,1,5,1,"nh"]],note:"Twelfth-fret harmonics up the strings, then three 5th-fret harmonics an octave higher. Every note belongs to Fmaj9."}]},{id:"tone",title:"Bright, clean and doubled",body:"This style lives on clean tones. Pitchfork's Ian Cohen sums up the bands that followed American Football as open-tuned Telecasters, capos and red and black flannels. A bright single-coil guitar into a clean amp with plenty of headroom lets every string of an open tuning ring on its own.\n\nAmerican Football used no pedals on their early recordings. The sound came from the open tunings, the pickup selector and layered parts, and they thickened the debut by doubling and tripling the guitar tracks. Mike Kinsella has said you can now buy a shimmer pedal to recreate what they did by hand. On stage today he plays Telecasters through a pair of Fender Deluxe Reverbs, while Steve Holmes moves between a Telecaster, a Jazzmaster for the lowered tunings and a Jaguar, through a Fender Twin Reverb that stays clean at volume.\n\nKeep the gain near zero. Use a light pick or your fingers, and let the dynamics come from your hands and the drums. A little chorus, delay and reverb gets you close live; doubling the part gets you closer on a recording.",cites:["s10","s3","s4"],blocks:[{type:"tone",id:"mwemo-clean",name:"Twinkly clean",kind:"electric",guitar:"Telecaster or Jazzmaster style",pickups:"Neck or middle position single-coil",strings:"10s",amp:"Clean American combo with plenty of headroom (Fender Deluxe Reverb or Twin Reverb type)",settings:{gain:2,bass:4,mid:5,treble:7,presence:6,reverb:3,delay:2},pedals:["chorus","delay","reverb"],demo:"mwemo-twinkle-arp",tips:["A bright single-coil guitar suits the style: Pitchfork sums up its players as open-tuned Telecasters with capos.","Keep the gain close to zero so each open string in the tuning rings clearly.","Double or triple the guitar part when you record. American Football thickened their debut this way.","Mike Kinsella has said a shimmer pedal can now recreate what the band did by layering by hand, so light chorus, delay and reverb are a fair live substitute.","Use a capo to move open-tuned shapes into a key that suits your voice.","Mike Kinsella keeps his Telecaster on the middle pickup position most of the time, and Steve Holmes saves his Jazzmaster for the lowered tunings."],cites:["s10","s3","s4","s7"],note:"Try the arpeggio with the neck pickup, then the middle position. The brighter one usually twinkles more."}]},{id:"form",title:"Builds, interludes and a trumpet",body:"Midwest songs often feel like suites. Guitar parts cycle and repeat, then shift: a verse in 4/4 can give way to an interlude in another meter or a long instrumental build. American Football added trumpet, which Steve Holmes called an organic addition; Steve Lamos first used it to sketch melody ideas for vocal lines.\n\nThe map below opens with the tapping figure, runs a quiet verse, builds over IVmaj7#11 and vi9 with tom-heavy drums, then breaks into a 7/8 interlude. A trumpet or second-guitar solo over the open Fmaj9 and Bbmaj7#11 closes the song. Vocals can sit out for long stretches; much math rock is purely instrumental.\n\nRearrange freely. Shorten the verse, extend the build, or let the interlude become the ending.",cites:["s1","s8","s6"],blocks:[{type:"form",name:"Midwest emo song map",sections:[{type:"intro",bars:4,chords:"Imaj9 - IVmaj7#11 -",groove:"mwemo-verse",fill:""},{type:"verse",bars:8,chords:"Imaj9 IVmaj7#11 vi9 V",groove:"mwemo-verse",fill:"f-snare8"},{type:"chorus",name:"Build",bars:8,chords:"IVmaj7#11 - vi9 iii7",groove:"build-tom",fill:"f-toms"},{type:"verse",bars:8,chords:"Imaj9 IVmaj7#11 vi9 V",groove:"mwemo-verse",fill:"f-snare8"},{type:"chorus",name:"Build",bars:8,chords:"IVmaj7#11 - vi9 iii7",groove:"build-tom",fill:"f-toms"},{type:"bridge",name:"7/8 interlude",bars:8,chords:"vi9 - IVmaj7#11 -",groove:"mwemo-seven",fill:""},{type:"solo",name:"Trumpet break",bars:8,chords:"Imaj9 - IVmaj7#11 -",groove:"mwemo-verse",fill:"f-build"},{type:"outro",bars:4,chords:"Imaj9 -",groove:"mwemo-verse",fill:"f-stop"}],note:"Verses and builds in 4/4, a 7/8 interlude, and a trumpet or guitar solo to close."}]},{id:"lyrics",title:"Nostalgia and place",body:"Midwest emo lyrics are introspective and often impressionistic: the Promise Ring's Davey von Bohlen wrote in an imagist style. American Football's songs dealt with teenage feelings, melancholy and the end of college, and the Urbana, Illinois, house on their album cover became a landmark for fans.\n\nWrite about a real place and a specific time: a basement, a street, the last week before everyone moved away. Place details do the emotional work, so the lines can stay plain and quiet. Nina Corcoran hears the record as being about the sadness of right now more than the past, so try writing the memory in the present tense.\n\nLeave space. With long instrumental sections, every sung line counts.",cites:["s1","s4","s9"],blocks:[{type:"lyrics",section:"verse",prompt:"Pick a house, street or room you used to spend time in. Write a verse in the present tense that names it and one thing you can still see there.",tips:['Name the place. Real street names and rooms beat general words like "home".',"Use one sensory detail per line: a sound, a light, a smell.","Short lines leave room for the guitars to answer."]}]},{id:"together",title:"Put it together",body:"You have the parts of a Midwest emo song: the FACGCE tuning and its open-string chords, a verse groove and a 7/8 interlude, twinkly arpeggios, tapping and harmonics, a bright doubled clean tone, a suite-like map and a lyric rooted in a place.\n\nWork through the checklist. Tune carefully between songs: American Football tell stories of spending minutes retuning between songs on stage, which is the price of a different tuning for every song. Write the tuning at the top of every chart and lyric sheet, and keep a clip-on tuner on the headstock while you write.",cites:["s8","s4"],blocks:[{type:"checklist",items:["Tune to FACGCE and play the six shapes, letting the open strings ring.","Play the twinkly arpeggio and the tapped riff at 90 BPM, then at 120.","Write a verse loop with Imaj9 and IVmaj7#11, and a build that sits on IV.","Add a 7/8 interlude to your song.","Dial a clean, bright tone and record the main guitar part twice.","Write a verse about a real place in the present tense and build the song from the map."]}]}]});